# UI Component Standards

## Back Navigation — Always Use `BackToHubButton`

Any button that navigates back to the hub or a parent view MUST use `BackToHubButton`.
Never create a custom back button with `ArrowLeft` + inline Tailwind.

```tsx
// ❌ BAD — custom back button
import { ArrowLeft } from 'lucide-react'
<button onClick={onBack} className="p-2 rounded-lg ...">
  <ArrowLeft className="w-5 h-5" />
</button>

// ✅ GOOD
import BackToHubButton from '@/components/ui/BackToHubButton'
<BackToHubButton onClick={onBack} />

// ✅ GOOD — custom label for non-hub destinations
<BackToHubButton onClick={onBack} label="Back to Inbox" />
```

`ArrowLeft` from lucide-react is only acceptable inside multi-step form wizard "Previous" buttons — not for page navigation.

---

## Cards — Always Use `Card` or `BlockCard`

Never use raw `<div>` with custom border/background/shadow classes as a card container.

```tsx
// ❌ BAD
<div className="bg-white rounded-2xl border border-gray-200 shadow-lg p-6">

// ✅ GOOD — general content card
import Card from '@/components/ui/Card'
<Card variant="elevated">

// ✅ GOOD — composable hub block
import BlockCard from '@/components/ui/BlockCard'
<BlockCard icon={ClipboardList} title="DOT Application" status="in-progress">
```

---

## Buttons — Always Use `Button`

Use the `Button` component for all interactive buttons. Inline button Tailwind is not allowed for primary/secondary/danger actions.

```tsx
// ❌ BAD
<button className="px-4 py-2 bg-teal-600 text-white rounded-lg hover:bg-teal-500">

// ✅ GOOD
import Button from '@/components/ui/Button'
<Button variant="primary">Save</Button>
<Button variant="secondary">Cancel</Button>
<Button variant="danger" size="sm">Delete</Button>
<Button isLoading={saving}>Save</Button>
```

`ghost` variant is acceptable for icon-only utility buttons inside complex UI (e.g. modal close X).

---

## Modals — Always Use `Modal` (NEVER hand-roll overlays)

Every overlay/dialog/picker in this app MUST use the shared `Modal` component from `@/components/ui/Modal`. This is **non-negotiable** because `Modal` handles three things that hand-rolled `fixed inset-0` divs always forget:

1. **Scroll lock** — sets `body.style.overflow = 'hidden'` on mount so the page behind doesn't scroll. Uses a nested-modal counter so the lock only lifts when the last modal closes.
2. **Portal** — uses `createPortal(content, document.body)` so the modal is outside every parent stacking context. This prevents the navbar or other `z-index` layers from rendering on top.
3. **z-index** — accepts a `zIndex` prop (default 1000). Nested modals just pass a higher value.

```tsx
// ❌ BAD — hand-rolled overlay (WILL have scroll bleed + navbar z-index issues)
<div className="fixed inset-0 z-[70] flex items-center justify-center">
  <div className="absolute inset-0 bg-black/50" onClick={onClose} />
  <div className="relative bg-white rounded-2xl max-w-2xl">
    {/* content */}
  </div>
</div>

// ✅ GOOD — use Modal + ModalHeader
import Modal, { ModalHeader } from '@/components/ui/Modal'

<Modal onClose={onClose} maxWidth="max-w-2xl">
  <ModalHeader title="Add Tools" subtitle="Pick industry-specific tools" onClose={onClose} />
  <div className="p-4">
    {/* content */}
  </div>
</Modal>
```

### If you think you need a custom overlay

You don't. The only exception is `LoadingScreen` (full-page takeover with no close button). Every other overlay — pickers, confirmations, previews, forms — goes through `Modal`.

### Checklist for new modals

- [ ] Uses `<Modal>` component (not `fixed inset-0`)
- [ ] Uses `<ModalHeader>` for title + close button (not a custom header)
- [ ] Sets `zIndex` if it can open on top of another modal
- [ ] Does NOT manually set `body.style.overflow` (Modal handles it)
- [ ] Does NOT use `createPortal` directly (Modal handles it)
